
THE GENERATION GAME

How Britain Learned to Skim, Fiddle, Claim and Call It Normal

A Systems Engineering Study of Informal Extraction, Selective Outrage and the Death of Plausible Deniability

Author: Martin Ollett
Classification: Everybody Knows a Bloke
Prepared by: Office of Missing Aluminium, Mysterious Overtime and Other Entirely Unrelated Coincidences
Date: September 2026

⸻

Abstract

Britain has developed a peculiar relationship with waste.

Waste is always appalling when somebody else is responsible for it.

The benefit claimant has received £20 they should not have received. Outrage.

The customer has stolen something from a supermarket. Outrage.

The employee has wasted company time. Outrage.

Somewhere else, however, perfectly serviceable material is being converted into scrap, Saturday overtime has mysteriously become unavoidable, company machinery is completing private work, stock is walking through the back gate, expenses are being claimed because apparently earning a substantial salary does not include the cost of getting oneself to work, and somebody who has spent thirty years taking whatever the system allowed is explaining that Britain must finally learn to live within its means.

Welcome to The Generation Game.

This paper examines what may be called the skim economy: the routine extraction of relatively concentrated private benefit from a cost dispersed across an employer, taxpayer, customer, colleague or institution.

The central mechanism is simple:

\text{Private Gain} + \text{Distributed Cost} + \text{Low Accountability}
\rightarrow
\text{Normalised Extraction}

The behaviour can be criminal, dishonest, technically permissible, institutionally sanctioned or simply culturally accepted. Those categories matter legally, but they need not produce different incentive structures.

The individual receives the benefit.

Everybody else receives a tiny fraction of the bill.

For decades, low observability allowed such practices to persist. Cash left little evidence. Stock systems were crude. Scrap disappeared. Overtime could be authorised by people receiving it. Expenses lived inside filing cabinets. Private work could pass through company machinery without automatically generating an audit trail.

Then came CCTV, electronic access systems, digital payments, smartphones, GPS, ERP software, public databases, searchable company records and transaction histories.

Suddenly everybody became terribly concerned about surveillance.

This paper argues that Britain is experiencing a collision between two environments: a low-observability economy in which informal extraction could become culturally normal, and a high-observability economy in which nearly everything eventually leaves evidence.

The Generation Game is therefore not simply about who took what.

It is about who taught whom that taking it was normal — and why everyone remains furious about the other bloke doing it.

