0. Welcome to the Generation Game

Every workplace has stories.

The bloke who knows exactly which skip contains the expensive metal.

The supervisor who discovers, late on Thursday afternoon, that an entirely unforeseeable production crisis means everyone will unfortunately have to come in Saturday at time-and-a-half.

The employee whose garage contains sixteen wheels, several tyres, three radiators, assorted power tools and approximately half a workshop despite him apparently never having purchased a car part in his life.

The manager whose mate turns up on Saturday carrying a private job which, by sheer cosmic coincidence, fits perfectly into the company’s machinery.

The shopper who scans one bag of compost and loads three into the car because the garden centre “charges enough anyway.”

And above the entire structure sits someone explaining that waste elsewhere in society has simply gone too far.

The Generation Game has remarkably simple rules.

Take something.

Make the individual benefit obvious but distribute the cost widely enough that nobody feels sufficiently harmed to intervene.

Give the behaviour a morally convenient name.

Then become incandescent with rage when somebody else extracts value through a mechanism you personally dislike.

This final rule is crucial.

The bloke carrying £200 of material through the back gate must remain furious about £20 of benefit fraud.

The employee deliberately stretching five days of work into six must complain about taxation.

The shopper stealing two bags of compost must have extremely strong opinions about shoplifting.

The organisation approving generous reimbursements must describe payments elsewhere as unaffordable.

Without selective moral accounting, the game becomes extremely difficult to play.

