1. The Fundamental Mathematics of Taking the Piss

The skim economy can be described as an incentive problem.

Suppose an individual can extract £100 while the cost is distributed across 10,000 other people.

The extractor experiences the whole £100.

Everyone else experiences one penny.

The incentive is concentrated.

The resistance is dispersed.

That asymmetry matters.

A more complete approximation might be written:

S \propto
\frac{B_p}
{C_p \times P_d \times P_c}

where B_p is private benefit, C_p is personally experienced cost, P_d is probability of detection and P_c is probability of meaningful consequence.

This is not intended as serious econometrics.

It is intended to formalise something virtually every person who has worked in a sufficiently chaotic organisation already understands.

If taking something is profitable, nobody personally appears to suffer very much, detection is unlikely and nothing meaningful happens when somebody gets caught, the behaviour becomes increasingly attractive.

Now introduce management participation.

The probability of consequence falls further.

This generates the first law of the Generation Game:

The most useful person to have participating in an informal extraction system is the person whose job is supposed to prevent it.

One worker taking material is misconduct.

A supervisor allowing it is a control failure.

Several employees and multiple managers participating is something else.

It is a local culture.

