2. Saturday: Where Aluminium Goes to Die

One of the author’s firsthand workplace accounts provides an unusually efficient demonstration.

Approximately seven people were involved.

Among them were the production manager and the manager responsible for fitting crews.

The material involved included perfectly serviceable sheets of aluminium approximately 10 mm thick.

On Saturdays, usable aluminium could find its way onto a wall saw and undergo an extraordinary industrial transformation.

It entered the process as valuable manufacturing stock.

It emerged as scrap.

This might seem economically disappointing until one remembers that scrap metal has another useful property:

it can be weighed in for cash.

The situation becomes more interesting when Saturday itself has economic value.

If production during the normal working week is sufficiently relaxed, delayed or badly managed, additional hours can become necessary.

Employees are then paid overtime.

The same period can subsequently be used to convert company material into privately valuable scrap.

One system has now produced two extraction channels.

The company buys additional labour.

The company loses usable material.

The participants potentially receive overtime income and scrap value.

This is not efficiency.

It is vertical integration.

The especially important feature of the example is management participation.

Production management should ordinarily form part of the control structure protecting labour efficiency, inventory and output.

Installation management provides another operational control point.

When people occupying both functions participate in the behaviour being controlled, the organisation chart becomes decorative.

On paper:

Management → supervises → workforce

In practice:

Everyone → supervises nobody → Saturday

A system cannot reliably police itself when the policeman is helping load the van.

