3. The £57-a-Week Bank of England

Corporate extraction is not the only version.

Sometimes the cost is transferred downwards instead of upwards.

At approximately fifteen or sixteen years old, the author was earning around £57 per week and was given one of Britain’s traditional introductory management positions:

sandwich lad.

The process was straightforward.

Collect the orders.

Telephone the sandwich shop.

Pay for everything.

Bring everything back.

Collect everybody’s money.

The flaw in this sophisticated financial architecture was that some adult workers simply did not pay.

Others took sandwiches belonging to somebody else.

The unpaid balance ultimately landed on the teenager.

This creates an almost perfect miniature of the skim economy.

The people most capable of absorbing the loss transferred it to the person least capable of absorbing it.

No offshore account was required.

No shell company.

No derivatives.

No elaborate fraud.

Just social hierarchy and the confidence that the lowest-paid person present would be easier to pressure than everybody else would be to challenge.

The underlying rule is:

Where possible, transfer dispersed organisational inconvenience onto somebody with less power than you.

At £57 per week, even a few missing lunches represented a material proportion of income.

To the adults taking them, each individual act was trivial.

The loss was small to the person creating it.

It was not small to the person receiving it.

