4. The Back Gate Economic Zone

Another workplace produced a different informal economy.

Stock could leave through a rear gate.

Components developed domestic applications never anticipated by their manufacturer.

Wheels, tyres, vehicle parts and other useful items somehow acquired new homes.

Private jobs could arrive during Saturday working.

A friend or acquaintance of an employee could bring in work.

Company equipment would perform the processing.

Payment could occur privately.

From a systems perspective, this is fascinating because the legitimate firm begins hosting a shadow enterprise inside itself.

The formal business supplies:

the building,

the machinery,

the electricity,

the maintenance,

the depreciation,

sometimes the labour,

and most importantly the risk.

The informal business retains the revenue.

One organisation therefore pays the capital costs while another, unofficial economic layer captures the margin.

Management accounting generally calls this undesirable.

British workplace culture has historically employed a broader vocabulary including:

“perks”,

“bits and pieces”,

“everyone does it”,

“they’ll never miss it”,

and the timeless:

“don’t worry about it.”

