5. The Company History That Refuses to Stay Simple

The author’s early sign-industry employment also provides a useful example of why organisational history can become considerably stranger than the legal name printed above the door.

Public Companies House records independently establish a continuing network of signage and engineering companies around Hull and Hessle, while the author’s firsthand account describes transfers of equipment, stock, personnel and operating activity through various stages of ownership and financial distress.

Gallagher Engineering (Hull) Limited, for example, has existed since 1997 and operates in the manufacture of metal structures. 

The significance for this paper is not to infer wrongdoing from corporate succession. Corporate restructurings, asset transfers and insolvencies can all occur legitimately.

The interesting systems observation is different.

To an employee standing beside a van, the boundary represented by a company name can be far less clear than it later appears in a filing cabinet.

A worker may be instructed:

load this,

move that,

take it there,

bring this back.

The worker sees management authority.

The accountant sees assets.

The insolvency practitioner sees legal ownership.

The employee with his hands around one end of a sheet of aluminium often sees:

“Steve said put it in the van.”

That difference between operational authority and legal ownership deserves considerably more attention in discussions of corporate accountability.

People at the bottom of organisations routinely execute decisions whose legal and financial significance they are in no position to assess.

Years later everybody asks why nobody challenged it.

Because at the time they were seventeen and holding the other end of the fucking sheet.

