6. The Great British Overtime Machine

Overtime is an excellent invention.

When demand exceeds normal capacity, employees can work additional hours and receive additional compensation.

Everybody wins.

There is, however, an alternative engineering implementation.

Step one: possess enough influence over normal productivity that unfinished work can accumulate.

Step two: announce that Saturday has sadly become unavoidable.

Step three: receive enhanced pay for solving the problem partially produced during Monday to Friday.

This converts inefficiency into personal income.

The mechanism matters because it reverses the normal incentive structure.

Ordinarily:

\text{Higher productivity} \rightarrow \text{Higher organisational value}

Under badly designed overtime incentives:

\text{Lower weekday productivity}
\rightarrow
\text{More additional hours}
\rightarrow
\text{Higher individual income}

Congratulations.

The organisation has constructed a system in which improving performance can reduce the employee’s earnings.

Nobody should then be surprised when miraculous productivity improvements appear immediately after overtime begins.

The Generation Game is full of arrangements like this.

They survive because organisations repeatedly insist on treating human behaviour as though incentives do not exist.

