7. One Bag of Compost, Three Bags Loaded

Not every example requires an employer.

Consider retail.

A person pays for one bag of compost and takes three.

The saving is immediate.

The cost disappears into the retailer’s inventory loss.

The justification arrives almost automatically.

“They make enough.”

This sentence deserves to be engraved above the entrance to the Museum of Petty Theft.

The same individual may never describe themselves as dishonest because the behaviour has undergone an important linguistic conversion.

Taking £12 directly from another person’s wallet is theft.

Taking £12 of goods from a large retailer becomes:

“beating the system.”

This distinction is psychologically convenient and economically meaningless to the balance sheet.

The critical variable is perceived victim distance.

The less visible the victim, the easier the extraction becomes to rationalise.

A multinational corporation has no face.

A government budget has no face.

A warehouse has no face.

A company’s pile of aluminium has no face.

A junior employee unfortunately does have a face, but apparently even that occasionally fails to help if he is carrying the sandwiches.

