9. “Taxpayer” Is Not Another Word for “Worker”

This brings us to one of Britain’s favourite rhetorical inventions.

The taxpayer.

The taxpayer is normally imagined wearing steel-toe boots, leaving the house at 6:30 a.m., paying PAYE and wondering why everyone else is spending his money.

There is a slight problem.

Taxation is considerably broader than employment income.

A person can receive substantial inherited wealth, never hold conventional employment and still pay large amounts through VAT, duties, investment taxation, property transactions and consumption.

Somebody purchasing extremely expensive cars, luxury goods and services can generate considerable tax receipts while having no conventional salary at all.

Conversely, somebody working full-time on low earnings may contribute relatively modest direct income tax.

Therefore:

\text{Worker} \neq \text{Taxpayer}

and

\text{Not Working} \neq \text{Not Paying Tax}

This sounds embarrassingly obvious because it is.

Nevertheless, political language repeatedly treats the categories as interchangeable.

Once that rhetorical substitution is made, social spending becomes:

money taken from workers and given to non-workers.

Actual public finance is considerably messier.

The Generation Game thrives on simplified moral categories.

Complex systems do not.

