10. Everyone Hates Fraud Except Their Fraud

Government itself provides a useful scale for the broader issue.

The Public Sector Fraud Authority cites a National Audit Office estimate that £55 billion to £81 billion was lost to fraud and error across the public sector in 2023–24. Importantly, this is not £55–81 billion of proven theft: the estimate explicitly combines fraud and error. 

That distinction is essential because Britain often talks about leakage as though money only travels incorrectly in one direction.

It doesn’t.

DWP’s official statistics for the financial year ending 2026 estimate £9.9 billion of benefit expenditure was overpaid through fraud and error.

The same statistics estimate £1.2 billion was underpaid because of official error. 

The system can therefore overpay people who should receive less and underpay people legally entitled to more.

Both are failures.

Only one generally produces a television programme with ominous music.

This asymmetric attention is itself part of the Generation Game.

When an individual receives too much:

Look at this outrage.

When an institution pays somebody too little:

Administrative issue.

When a claimant owes government money:

Debt recovery.

When government owes a claimant money:

Please allow twenty-eight working days.

The numbers differ.

The moral vocabulary differs even more.

