11. The Human Consequence of “Administrative Error”

This distinction is not academic to the author.

Following redundancy in 2008, after working continuously from school age, the author describes being sanctioned and surviving for months largely on a small hardship payment.

According to his account, he was subsequently informed that his contribution history meant he had been placed in the wrong category.

By then the consequences were not spreadsheet entries.

Housing was lost.

A relationship collapsed.

The money did not arrive when it was required.

Employment was subsequently found.

The retrospective problem did not reconstruct the months already lived.

This is why measuring public systems only through aggregate leakage is inadequate.

A £1,000 error does not have one fixed social cost.

For somebody with substantial savings, it may be irritating.

For somebody with £4.17 left, it can alter the trajectory of their life.

The state therefore faces the same systems-engineering problem as every employer discussed earlier:

Who receives the benefit of an error, and who carries the consequence?

