12. Then They Installed Cameras

And now we reach the great historical disruption.

For decades, informal extraction benefited from poor observability.

A loading bay was a loading bay.

A stock room was a room full of things somebody vaguely remembered purchasing.

Cash existed without a database.

A company vehicle went somewhere because Dave said it needed to.

A pallet contained approximately enough.

A timesheet had whatever somebody wrote on it.

Then technology committed the ultimate workplace betrayal.

It remembered things.

CCTV.

Access logs.

Digital inventory.

Automatic number-plate recognition.

Card payments.

Electronic payroll.

Vehicle telematics.

Mobile phones.

Photographs with timestamps.

ERP systems.

Searchable public expenditure.

Companies House available from the sofa.

Suddenly behaviour that previously required an auditor, several filing cabinets and the patience of a saint could be reconstructed by somebody sitting on the toilet.

This produced a fascinating cultural reaction.

People who had spent decades explaining that anyone behaving properly had nothing to worry about suddenly developed extremely sophisticated views about surveillance.

Cameras appeared above loading bays.

And apparently civilisation itself was under attack.

Of course, privacy concerns can be entirely legitimate.

But in some workplaces the speed with which philosophical objections appeared was remarkably correlated with the disappearance of things through particular doors.

