13. The Death of Plausible Deniability

The smartphone did something politically and organisationally important.

It reduced the cost of evidence.

Previously:

“You’ll never prove that.”

Now:

“There’s a video.”

Previously:

“Nobody knows what they claimed.”

Now:

“Here’s the downloadable spreadsheet.”

Previously:

“That company has nothing to do with that one.”

Now:

“Give me thirty seconds on Companies House.”

Previously:

“That’s just how it happened.”

Now:

“Here’s the timestamp.”

Power has historically benefited from informational asymmetry.

The supervisor knows more than the worker.

The company knows more than the customer.

Government knows more than the citizen.

The institution has the records.

The individual has a memory.

Digitisation does not eliminate that asymmetry, but it has weakened it dramatically.

The same technology that allows organisations to monitor workers increasingly allows workers, customers and citizens to inspect organisations.

Scrutiny can now travel upwards.

That is the bit nobody planned for.

