14. The Actual Generation Game

This paper’s title does not claim that everybody born within a particular twenty-year period is dishonest.

That would be statistically absurd and intellectually boring.

The generational question is about cultural transmission.

A sixteen-year-old entering a workplace does not arrive knowing its unofficial rules.

They learn them.

They learn which breaks are actually twenty minutes longer than stated.

They learn which material is apparently free.

They learn whether overtime is genuine.

They learn whether taking damaged stock home is permitted.

Then they learn that “damaged” is a surprisingly flexible engineering classification.

They learn who can use the machine for private work.

They learn whether managers care.

Most importantly, they learn what everybody else regards as normal.

If the senior workforce says:

“Everyone does it,”

the new employee has been taught something considerably more powerful than a written procedure.

They have been taught the real operating system.

Some copy it.

Some refuse.

Some eventually become supervisors and reproduce it.

That is the Generation Game.

Not genetics.

Transmission.

