15. Why the Game Eventually Stops Working

Informal extraction is easiest when four conditions exist:

low observability,

dispersed victims,

weak accountability,

and cultural permission.

Modern systems attack the first condition aggressively.

Digital systems also make the second weaker because dispersed losses can increasingly be aggregated and attributed.

What once looked like:

“only a bit”

can become:

£600 this month,

£7,200 this year,

£72,000 across ten sites.

Data turns anecdotes into distributions.

That changes organisational behaviour.

It also changes politics.

The public no longer has to accept “that’s just what it costs” quite as readily when databases reveal what things actually cost.

The same principle should apply everywhere.

Benefits.

Procurement.

Corporate expenses.

Inventory shrinkage.

Overtime.

Tax avoidance and evasion.

Parliamentary expenditure.

Executive perks.

Retail theft.

Administrative underpayment.

Public-sector fraud.

Private-sector fraud.

The sensible systems question is not:

Which social group should we be angry with this week?

It is:

Where does the system permit concentrated private benefit while dispersing the resulting cost so widely that meaningful resistance disappears?

Find those points.

Then redesign them.

