16. Stop Skimming Off the Fucking Top

Britain does not suffer from one single giant man hiding behind the Treasury carrying a sack labelled MONEY.

Leakage is distributed.

Some is enormous.

Some is microscopic.

Some is criminal.

Some is technically lawful.

Some is bureaucratic error.

Some is poor system design.

Some is a bloke taking aluminium.

Some is somebody knowingly claiming money to which they are not entitled.

Some is an organisation failing to pay somebody money to which they are entitled.

Some is unnecessary procurement.

Some is deliberately created overtime.

Some is a sandwich nicked from the kid earning £57 a week.

The amounts and legal categories differ.

The underlying cultural problem often does not:

My little bit doesn’t count.

It does.

Multiply “my little bit” by millions of people, thousands of companies and hundreds of institutions and eventually Britain develops an economy in which everyone is furious about everybody else’s extraction while defending their own as completely reasonable.

That is not fundamentally a left-wing problem.

It is not fundamentally a right-wing problem.

It is not confined to rich people, poor people, employees, businesses, politicians or benefit recipients.

It is an incentive-design problem wrapped inside a cultural one.

The older low-observability economy made it surprisingly easy to sustain.

The modern digital economy increasingly does not.

The cupboard now has an inventory system.

The gate has a camera.

The van has GPS.

The payment has a transaction ID.

The company has a searchable filing history.

The expense has a downloadable spreadsheet.

And practically everyone has a camera in their pocket.

The age of “nobody will ever know” is ending.

Perhaps the appropriate response is not another national campaign identifying whichever group is currently fashionable to blame.

Perhaps it is considerably simpler.

Stop nicking the sandwiches.

Stop manufacturing the overtime.

Stop turning good aluminium into beer money.

Stop helping yourself to stock.

Stop designing systems in which the people authorising expenditure personally benefit from spending more.

Stop treating money owed by institutions as morally different from money owed to them.

Stop calling your extraction an entitlement while describing somebody else’s as theft.

In other words:

Stop skimming off the fucking top.

Because after several generations of playing the game, the most inconvenient development imaginable has finally arrived.

Everyone can see the scoreboard.
